The final match of the [[1970 FIFA World Cup]] was held on Sunday, 21 June, in the [[Estadio Azteca]] in [[Mexico City]], between [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] and [[Italy national football team|Italy]]. It marked the first time that two former world champions met in a final; Italy had previously won the World Cup in [[1934 FIFA World Cup|1934]] and [[1938 FIFA World Cup|1938]], while Brazil won in [[1958 FIFA World Cup|1958]] and [[1962 FIFA World Cup|1962]].

== Road to the final==
Before the finals in Mexico, Brazil had to play qualifying matches against [[Colombia national football team|Colombia]], [[Venezuela national football team|Venezuela]] and [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]]. Brazil was far superior, winning all six games, scoring twenty-three goals and conceding only two. The last match of the qualifying round, which Brazil won against Paraguay 1–0, had the largest official audience ever recorded for a football match, with 183,341 spectators in Brazil's [[Maracanã Stadium]]. In total, the Brazilian team won all 12 games, scoring 42 goals and conceding only 8.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=GGgVawPscysC&dat=19700622&printsec=frontpage&hl=en|title=Brazil outplay Italy and take Jules Rimet Trophy outright|date=22 June 1970|work=Glasgow Herald (Page 4)|access-date=30 April 2014|archive-date=3 July 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200703090441/https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=GGgVawPscysC&dat=19700622&printsec=frontpage&hl=en|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/62712350|title=Pele and 1970: How the greatest player of all time cemented his legend|date=31 October 2022|work=BBC Sport|accessdate=16 November 2022|archive-date=1 November 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221101030822/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/62712350|url-status=live}}</ref>

