==Post-match==
[[File:Pele celebrating 1970.jpg|thumb|Pelé celebrating after winning the match. It was his last World Cup as he would retire from the national team one year later<ref>[https://libero.pe/futbol-internacional/1572907-pele-jugo-ultimo-partido-seleccion-brasilena-yugoslavia-efemerides-maracana-rivelino-gerson Un día como hoy Pelé jugó su último partido con la selección brasileña] on Libero, 18 Jul 2020 {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201002034402/https://libero.pe/futbol-internacional/1572907-pele-jugo-ultimo-partido-seleccion-brasilena-yugoslavia-efemerides-maracana-rivelino-gerson |date=2 October 2020 }}</ref>]]
With this third win after their [[1958 FIFA World Cup|1958]] and [[1962 FIFA World Cup|1962]] World Cup victories, Brazil became the world's most successful national football team at that time, surpassing both Italy and [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]], who each had two championships. The third title earned Brazil the right to retain the [[FIFA World Cup Trophy#Jules Rimet Trophy|Jules Rimet Trophy]] permanently;<ref name=bwcwstp>{{cite news |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=uplSAAAAIBAJ&pg=6097%2C2070851 |newspaper=St. Petersburg Times |agency=Associated Press |title=Brazil |date=22 June 1970 |page=1C |archive-date=18 October 2022 |access-date=6 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221018124729/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=uplSAAAAIBAJ&pg=6097,2070851 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/news/brazil-heroes-1970-relive-their-days-glory-73382 |title=Brazil's heroes of 1970 relive their days of glory |work=FIFA.com |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |date=10 June 2000 |access-date=9 June 2021|archiveurl =https://web.archive.org/web/20190205191934/https://www.fifa.com/news/brazil-heroes-1970-relive-their-days-glory-73382 |archivedate=5 February 2019}}</ref> it was stolen in 1983 while on display in [[Rio de Janeiro]] and never recovered.

Thirty-eight-year-old Brazilian coach [[Mário Zagallo]] became the first footballer to win the World Cup as a player (1958, 1962) and a coach, as well the second youngest coach to win a World Cup, after [[Alberto Suppici]] in [[1930 FIFA World Cup|1930]]. [[Pelé]] ended his World Cup playing career as the competition's first (and as of 2026, only) three-time winner.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.goal.com/en/news/60/south-america/2010/10/21/2176031/70-facts-about-brazil-legend-football-icon-pele-on-his-70th |title=70 Facts About Brazil Legend Football Icon Pele On His 70th Birthday |work=Goal.com |date=21 October 2010 |access-date=8 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101023020649/http://www.goal.com/en/news/60/south-america/2010/10/21/2176031/70-facts-about-brazil-legend-football-icon-pele-on-his-70th |archive-date=23 October 2010 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

=== Legacy ===
Historically, this 1970 Brazil squad is generally revered as the best World Cup team.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Molinaro |first1=John |title=THE TOP 10 WORLD CUP TEAMS OF ALL-TIME |url=https://www.sportsnet.ca/soccer/ranking-top-10-world-cup-teams-time/ |website=Sportsnet |publisher=Sportsnet |access-date=2026-07-20 |archive-date=20 July 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260720111723/https://www.sportsnet.ca/soccer/ranking-top-10-world-cup-teams-time/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

